---
import LandingLayout from "../layouts/LandingLayout.astro";
import { LandingActions } from "../components/LandingActions";
---

<LandingLayout>
  <div class="land">
    <header class="land-wrap land-bar">
      <a class="land-mark" href="/">EasyCarCare</a>
      <LandingActions client:load variant="bar" />
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="land-wrap land-hero">
        <div>
          <p class="land-kicker">For everyday drivers</p>
          <h1>Know the next useful thing your car needs.</h1>
          <p class="land-lede">
            EasyCarCare turns a vehicle and its mileage into a plain-language
            plan. No shop jargon, and no guessing whether the oil change was
            last spring or last year.
          </p>
          <LandingActions client:load />
        </div>
        <aside class="land-preview" aria-label="Example of a maintenance plan">
          <p class="land-kicker">A plan, in plain language</p>
          <p class="land-preview-car">2018 Honda Civic</p>
          <p class="land-preview-miles">54,200 miles</p>
          <article class="land-task land-task-soon">
            <div>
              <h2>Oil change</h2>
              <p>Due in about 500 miles</p>
            </div>
            <p class="status">Due soon</p>
          </article>
          <article class="land-task">
            <div>
              <h2>Tire rotation</h2>
              <p>Due in about 3,800 miles</p>
            </div>
            <p class="status">Upcoming</p>
          </article>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="land-wrap land-section">
        <h2>Plain answers, not a repair manual</h2>
        <p>
          Add a car. EasyCarCare tells you what matters next, then keeps the
          record when the work is done.
        </p>
        <div class="land-grid">
          <article class="card">
            <h3>The next useful step</h3>
            <p>
              Overdue, due soon, and upcoming stay in sentences a driver can act
              on.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>A plan that starts itself</h3>
            <p>
              A new vehicle gets a starter schedule from its mileage. You do not
              have to look up intervals first.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>A record you can trust</h3>
            <p>
              Log the date, mileage, and an optional cost. The next due point
              moves forward with the work.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="land-wrap land-section">
        <h2>Three steps, then you can see the plan</h2>
        <ol class="land-steps">
          <li>
            <h3>Create an account</h3>
            <p>Your vehicles and service history stay private to you.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Add the car and its mileage</h3>
            <p>Year, make, model, and the odometer. A VIN is optional.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Follow the next step</h3>
            <p>
              Update the mileage as you drive, and log the service when it is
              finished.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="land-wrap land-section">
        <h2>A starter plan, already written</h2>
        <p>
          New vehicles begin with ordinary care. It is general guidance, not a
          manufacturer schedule.
        </p>
        <ul class="land-services">
          <li>Oil change</li>
          <li>Tire rotation</li>
          <li>Tire-pressure check</li>
          <li>Air-filter replacement</li>
          <li>Brake inspection</li>
          <li>Battery check</li>
          <li>Coolant inspection</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="land-close">
        <div class="land-wrap">
          <h2>Add a vehicle. See what it needs next.</h2>
          <p>
            The same plan works on a phone in the driveway and on a desktop at
            the kitchen table.
          </p>
          <LandingActions client:load />
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</LandingLayout>
